; ACCEPTANCE: a program reaches the JVM's objects, with every declaration
; GENERATED by gauntlet/stdlib/jvm.go from the runtime itself.
;
; THE RUNTIME IS THE MANIFEST. Go ships `api/go1*.txt` and the Windows SDK ships
; headers; the JDK ships neither and needs neither, because `jrt:/` plus
; reflection IS the JDK. So this program's declarations cannot be wrong about
; what exists — only about what we can do with it, which is what it checks.
;
; FOUR THINGS, and each is a refusal that had to be answered.
;
;   `new`               `(ju.StringJoiner ", ")` emits `new java.util.StringJoiner(", ")`.
;                       Java's constructor idiom is a KEYWORD, not a function, so
;                       4,138 of them exist where Go has a handful of `NewT` — and
;                       the survey has to skip the ones on an abstract class,
;                       which `getConstructors` lists anyway.
;
;   THE COERCION        `StringJoiner` takes a `CharSequence` and we hold a
;                       `string`. `(implements string java-lang-CharSequence …)`
;                       is what makes that legal here, and the emitted Java has
;                       NO conversion syntax at all — `⟦coerce⟧ = id`, exactly as
;                       on Go. The difference is where the relation comes from:
;                       Go's had to be computed structurally and then filtered by
;                       `go build`, which refused 182 of 1,651 candidates. THIS
;                       HOST DECLARES ITS OWN RELATION, so `extends` and
;                       `implements` are READ and there is nothing to guess.
;
;   THE OVERLOAD        `add` is one name at one arity here, but `Random.nextInt`
;                       is two and `nextInt2` is the second. `tg.Prims` is keyed
;                       by NAME ALONE, so Java's overloading is a real format
;                       refusal — overloading.md §3 calls `Println`/`Println2`/
;                       `Println3` "a target-file wart" with ONE live instance,
;                       and this host has 5,446.
;
;   A METHOD CHAIN      `add` returns the joiner, so the receiver of the next
;                       call is the result of the last — which is what makes a
;                       method a SOURCE in the obtainable fixed point, and not
;                       just a consumer. Leaving that out understated Go by ten
;                       points until this survey was written.
;
; It prints `a, b, c` and then 30, which is `new java.util.Random(42).nextInt(100)`
; — a value the JVM specifies, so it is checkable against the host rather than
; against ourselves.

(use java/java-util as ju)
(use java/java-util/StringJoiner as SJ)
(use java/java-util/Random as Random)
(use io)

(export main)

(def main (fn ()
  (seq
    (io.print-line
      (SJ.toString (SJ.add (SJ.add (SJ.add (ju.StringJoiner ", ") "a") "b") "c")))
    (io.print-int (Random.nextInt2 (ju.Random2 42) 100)))))
